 W(2, PARAM, 'Write `count_above(values, limit)` that returns how many values are above `limit`. Print `count_above([45, 82, 67, 90, 38], 60)`.', '# def count_above(values, limit):\n', 'def count_above(values, limit):\n    count = 0\n    for v in values:\n        if v > limit:\n            count += 1\n    return count\n\nprint(count_above([45, 82, 67, 90, 38], 60))', "A function can take a whole list as one argument, plus a second value to compare with."),
 W(2, WHAT, 'Write `clean_name(text)` that returns the text with the spaces at the ends removed, in title case. Use it in a list comprehension on `[" asha ", "RAVI"]` and print the list.', '# def clean_name(text):\n', 'def clean_name(text):\n    return text.strip().title()\n\nprint([clean_name(n) for n in [" asha ", "RAVI"]])', "Write the cleaning steps once in a function, then reuse them for every name."),
 # ---------- Challenge (same chapter, combined) ----------
 W(3, RET, 'Write `is_prime(n)` that returns `True` or `False` (numbers below 2 are not prime). Use it in a list comprehension to print the primes below 20.', '# def is_prime(n):\n', 'def is_prime(n):\n    if n < 2:\n        return False\n    for d in range(2, n):\n        if n % d == 0:\n            return False\n    return True\n\nprint([n for n in range(20) if is_prime(n)])', "`return False` leaves as soon as a divisor is found. Only if the loop finishes is the number prime."),
 W(3, SEVERAL, 'Write `summary(marks)` that returns the average (rounded to 1 decimal), the highest, the lowest and how many passed (35 or more). For `[45, 82, 67, 90, 38]`, unpack it and print `Avg 64.4 | High 90 | Low 38 | Passed 5`.', '# def summary(marks):\n', 'def summary(marks):\n    average = round(sum(marks) / len(marks), 1)\n    passed = len([m for m in marks if m >= 35])\n    return average, max(marks), min(marks), passed\n\navg, high, low, passed = summary([45, 82, 67, 90, 38])\nprint(f"Avg {avg} | High {high} | Low {low} | Passed {passed}")', "One function can return a whole report as a tuple, and the caller unpacks it into four clear names."),
 W(3, DEFAULT, 'Write `price_after(price, discount=0, gst=18)`: take off `discount`% first, then add `gst`%, and round to 2 decimals. Print `price_after(1000)`, `price_after(1000, discount=10)` and `price_after(1000, gst=5, discount=20)` with one `print()`.', '# def price_after(price, discount=0, gst=18):\n', 'def price_after(price, discount=0, gst=18):\n    after_discount = price - price * discount / 100\n    return round(after_discount + after_discount * gst / 100, 2)\n\nprint(price_after(1000), price_after(1000, discount=10), price_after(1000, gst=5, discount=20))', "Keyword arguments let you change any default you want, in any order, and leave the others as they are."),
 T(3, RET, 'def size(x):\n    if x > 10:\n        return "big"\n    if x > 5:\n        return "medium"\n\nprint(size(12), size(7), size(2))', "For 2, neither `if` is true and the function reaches its end without a `return`, so it gives `None`."),
 F(3, RET, "This code should print `False`, but it prints `False` and then `None`. Fix it.", 'def is_even(n):\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        return True\n    print(False)\n\nprint(is_even(3))', 'def is_even(n):\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        return True\n    return False\n\nprint(is_even(3))', "Printing inside the function is not the same as returning. The caller only gets what you `return`."),
 W(3, BUILTIN, 'Write `to_celsius(f)` that returns `(f - 32) * 5 / 9` rounded to 1 decimal, and `report(temps)` that uses it to return a list of Celsius values. Print `report([98.6, 32, 212])`.', '# def to_celsius(f):\n# def report(temps):\n', 'def to_celsius(f):\n    return round((f - 32) * 5 / 9, 1)\n\ndef report(temps):\n    return [to_celsius(t) for t in temps]\n\nprint(report([98.6, 32, 212]))', "Small functions can call each other: `report()` reuses `to_celsius()` for every value."),
 W(3, PARAM, 'Write `percent(part, whole)` that returns `part / whole * 100` rounded to 1 decimal. Call it with keyword arguments in the other order, `percent(whole=200, part=50)`, and print the result.', '# def percent(part, whole):\n', 'def percent(part, whole):\n    return round(part / whole * 100, 1)\n\nprint(percent(whole=200, part=50))', "With keyword arguments the order does not matter: each value goes to the parameter with that name."),
